## Introduction

In this lab, I worked with xv6's page tables to optimize memory usage and system call performance. I implemented features like speeding up system calls, printing page tables, and using superpages for large memory allocations.

---

## Lab Tasks

### Inspect a User-Process Page Table
I analyzed the page table of a user process and explained the purpose of each page table entry and its permission bits.

---

### Speed Up System Calls
I sped up the `getpid()` system call by sharing data between userspace and the kernel using a read-only page.

---

### Print a Page Table
I implemented a function to print the contents of a page table in a readable format. This helped me visualize how page tables are structured.

---

### Use Superpages
I modified xv6 to use superpages (2MB pages) for large memory allocations, reducing the number of page table entries and improving performance.

---

## Building and Running xv6

1. **Prerequisites**:  
   - I installed the RISC-V toolchain from [riscv-gnu-toolchain](https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain).
   - I installed QEMU for RISC-V (`riscv64-softmmu`).

2. **Build xv6**:
   ```bash
   $ make qemu

Testing and Grading
I used pgtbltest to test my implementations.
I ran usertests -q to ensure my changes didn't break other functionality.
